feat(core-literature): replace the single-byte PDF check with four (v0.15.0)

The old check read five bytes and stopped. A five-byte file passed, and a
truncated download passed too. That is worse than a reported failure: the file
lands in pdf/, the analyst downstream opens it, finds nothing readable, and the
paper gets blamed for being unparseable when it was never downloaded.

validate_pdf_bytes now applies four checks, each returning a distinct reason so
the failure report names the actual problem:

1. `%PDF-` header — catches an HTML error page wearing a .pdf extension.
2. Minimum size — catches a stub that is technically a header and nothing else.
3. `%%EOF` within the last 2 KB — catches truncation. The window matters
   because writers append metadata after the marker.
4. `/Type` or `trailer` present — catches a header glued onto foreign bytes.
   PDF 1.5+ cross-reference streams still carry `/Type`, so this does not
   reject them.

The same function now backs both paths. Previously `valid_pdf` checked five
bytes on disk while the download path checked the header only — two different
standards, which meant a corrupt file left by an interrupted run was accepted
as "already downloaded" and never refetched.

All four new reasons are terminal: retrying a URL cannot fix bytes the server
sent.

Frontend: the card printed machine codes (`pdf_truncated`) at the operator.
`describeDownloadFailure` maps each code to a sentence, matched by prefix so
`unexpected_content_type:text/html` still resolves. The raw code is kept
alongside the translation, but only when a translation exists — otherwise a
reason already written in prose would print twice. The card's description of
the checks was also stale (it still said "four checks" and listed the old
ones) and now matches what the script actually does.

Also swept research-topic for demo/mock/TODO residue: none found.

Tests: 13 Python (stdlib unittest, no new dependency) + 3 frontend.
Verification: frontend 276/276 (24 files), Python 13/13, `vite build` and
`tsc --noEmit` clean.
